t was held for 25 years by Republican  buck McKeon and then for four years by  Republican Steve Knight they call it a  pur purple district but it's  a Republican held district at the  Congressional level for a really really  long time and then it went to Katie Hill  and Katie Hill had to resign and there  was a blowback effect to some degree  that has nothing to do with what we  would expect to see in the fall so  that's number one  Republicans are seeing the headline as  hey a Republican won a seat that was  just held by a Democrat this is great  for Republicans the real story is that  Katie Hill had taken the seat that had  been held by Republicans for a really  long time and it doesn't tell us that  Trump is going to start winning blue  states in 2020 then you get to  Wisconsin's seventh I actually think  that Wisconsin's seventh although it got  less attention than could the California  25th is more relevant for thinking about  2020 if you look at the results of the  Wisconsin's seventh special election  just recently and compare it to how  those areas voted in 2016 there was  really no major shift if you look at the  elements that make up the Wisconsin  seventh and you look at how did they  vote on Trump versus Hillary in 2016 and  how did they vote for Tiffany versus the  Democratic challenger in that recent  special election you could say well  maybe I believe it's pronounced an Ida  or an ADA in Ada County that might be a  flip that came within twenty votes in  the judicial election that just took  place it's gonna be pretty similar to  2016 so Wisconsin's 7th is potentially  more consequential when thinking about  2020 because any small change in  Wisconsin could flip the state when you  look at California you can argue about  California's 25th as a sort of  guideposts for what might happen bigger  picture in California but Trump's not  gonna win California no matter what  right Katie Hill resigned she was a  Democrat now a Republican won been a  Republican state for decades before it  was Katie it doesn't matter because  Biden's going to win California in  Wisconsin even these small shifts the  margin was small enough in Wisconsin I  don't remember exactly what I think was  about 20,000 votes  that is a state that if the wind blows  one way versus the other if older voters  abandoned Trump and go to Biden the way  we've been seeing you could actually get  a different result where Donald Trump  doesn't win Wisconsin so I don't think  either tells us what is in store for  November but if I were to look at one of  them as maybe being a more impactful  signal it would be looking at  Wisconsin's seventh not California's  25th
 

